/* Alpine drainage erosion — index1-5-alpine, 2026-10-23.
   Runs once, at load, on the watershed's baked height grid (a tensor-product grid xs × zs, so cells
   may be 12, 20, 24, 40 or 140 units across; every distance and area below is the real one).
   1. Spurs: a warped ridged-noise layer seeds secondary ribs on the mountain mass.
   2. Drainage: priority-flood (+ε) gives every node a downhill path to the rivers or the edge;
      steepest-descent receivers and the drained area A follow from it.
   3. Incision: implicit stream power (Braun & Willett 2013, n = 1, m = .5) lowers every node toward
      its receiver by K·√A/d; big catchments cut deep V valleys, small ones fine gullies, and every
      cut runs down the fall line by construction. Talus relaxation then lays the over-steepened
      channel walls back to a rock-slope limit, so valleys are V-shaped and divides stay sharp.
   4. Crests: an unsharp mask lifts the divides the erosion leaves into knife-edge ridgelines.
   Nodes are weighted by how much of their height is mountain mass (0 on river beds, banks and the
   canyon's support), so the beds, banks and camera route are untouched: the rivers are the base
   level everything drains to. Outputs: the height change, and per-node flow (log drained area),
   scree (talus deposit / foot of steep ground) and rib (local convexity) for the material.
   Deterministic; no Math.random. */

const fract = x => x - Math.floor(x);
